# How JavaScript Runs Code

We can now get JavaScript onto a page and see what it produces. The next question is the order in which the engine does things. By default, the JavaScript engine runs our code **from top to bottom**, one statement after another. This is called **sequential execution**, and it is the rule that everything else in this module builds on.

## Top to Bottom

If we write three `console.log` statements, the engine runs the first, then the second, then the third:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log('A');
console.log('B');
console.log('C');
```

The output is `A`, then `B`, then `C`, in that order. Try swapping two of the lines and running the cell again: the output order follows the order of the code, always.

## Several Pieces of JavaScript in One Page

The same rule applies across a whole HTML file. If a page has more than one piece of JavaScript in it, the engine works through them in the order they appear in the document. Suppose the first `<script>` element logs `A` and a later one logs `B`; the overall output is `A` and then `B`.

Here is a concrete version of that:

```html
<html>
  <head>
    <script>
      console.log("first");
      console.log("second");
    </script>
    <script>
      console.log("third");
    </script>
  </head>
</html>
```

The output is `first`, then `second`, then `third`. The JavaScript engine effectively "glues" the code from all script elements together and runs it in the order it appears in the document.

## Exceptions Are Coming

Top to bottom execution is the default but later in this module we will see some exceptions to this rule, including:

- **Comments**, which are ignored by the engine and can be used to disable code.
- **Conditional execution**, where a block of statements runs only when something is true.
- **Loops**, where a block of statements runs over and over.
- **Function calls**, where the engine jumps into a chunk of code defined somewhere else, runs it, and then comes back.

None of those break the rule so much as bend it: inside each piece, execution is still top to bottom. For now, whenever you are trying to work out what a script does, start by reading it the way the engine does, from the first line downward.

## Exercises

1. Reorder the three lines below so that the output reads `Ann`, then `Arbor`, then `Michigan`.

    ```{javascript}
    console.log('Michigan');
    console.log('Ann');
    console.log('Arbor');
    ```
    ```+solution
    console.log('Ann');
    console.log('Arbor');
    console.log('Michigan');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'Ann,Arbor,Michigan').run('The three lines should log `Ann`, then `Arbor`, then `Michigan`', 'Logged the three values in order');
    ```

2. Without changing or moving the two lines that are already there, add one line so that the output reads `start`, `middle`, `end`.

    ```{javascript}
    console.log('start');
    console.log('end');
    ```
    ```+solution
    console.log('start');
    console.log('middle');
    console.log('end');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(console.history().map(entry => entry.text).join(',')).should('equal', 'start,middle,end').run('Add a line logging `middle` between the two existing lines', 'Put the new line in the right place');
    ```

3. A page has more than one `<script>` element. In what order does the code run?

    ```{mcq}
    An HTML page contains these three script elements, in this order in the document:

    ~~~html
    <script>
      console.log('one');
    </script>
    <script>
      console.log('two');
      console.log('three');
    </script>
    <script>
      console.log('four');
    </script>
    ~~~

    What does the page log?
    correct: Correct! The engine works through the script elements in document order, and runs the statements inside each one from top to bottom, exactly as if the three elements were a single program.
    wrong: The engine effectively glues the script elements together in the order they appear in the document. Look back at "Several Pieces of JavaScript in One Page".
    [x] `one`, `two`, `three`, `four`
      correct: Right: document order across the elements, and top to bottom within each one.
      wrong: The engine takes the elements in the order they appear and runs each one from top to bottom, which produces exactly this order.
    [ ] `one`, `four`, `two`, `three`
      wrong: The engine does not skip ahead to a later script element. It finishes one before starting the next.
    [ ] `four`, `three`, `two`, `one`
      wrong: Nothing runs in reverse. The engine starts at the first script element and works downward.
    [ ] The order is not predictable, because each script element runs on its own
      wrong: Script elements are not separate programs running independently. The engine runs them in document order, one after another.
    ```

